 ## Overview
-Hardware-based Direct Volume Ray Casting offloads the computationally intensive tasks of ray casting, shading, and compositing to dedicated programmable Digital Signal Processors (DSPs), decoupling these operations from general-purpose processors and geometry units. Unlike software implementations that rely on general-purpose CPU or GPU shaders, this method uses specialized DSPs to compute light-material interactions for volume elements with high throughput, as seen in the VIRIM architecture [S1]. It is distinct from tree-based hardware methods like the Ray Projection Tree by using sequential scanline processing per DSP rather than parallel hierarchical combination [S1, S2].
+Hardware-based Direct Volume Ray Casting offloads the computationally intensive tasks of ray casting, shading, and compositing to dedicated programmable Digital Signal Processors (DSPs), decoupling these operations from general-purpose processors and geometry units [S2]. Unlike software implementations that rely on general-purpose CPU or GPU shaders, this method uses specialized DSPs to compute light-material interactions for volume elements with high throughput, as implemented in the VIRIM architecture [S2]. It is distinct from tree-based hardware methods like the Ray Projection Tree by using sequential scanline processing per DSP rather than parallel hierarchical combination [S2, S3].
 
 ## How it works
